I. Environment & Project Setup
1. Create a Django project. Django auto-generates core files: `manage.py`, `settings.py`, `urls.py`, `views.py`.
2. Activate Python virtual environment and confirm Django is installed. Configure PyCharm run configuration with `runserver` command.
3. Fix port conflict: Use `netstat -ano | findstr ":8000"` to get PID of occupying process. Kill process with `taskkill /F /PID [PID]`. Alternative: start server on another port `runserver 127.0.0.1:8001`.
4. Browser cache tip: Press `Ctrl + F5` for hard refresh after code changes, to avoid loading cached old page.

II. URL Route & Basic View
1. urls.py (URL config): Use path(url_string, view_function) to map web address to handler function.
2. View functions are defined in views.py. Every view takes one required argument request, which represents the HTTP request from user.
3. First response method: HttpResponse("content"). Return plain text, simple HTML tags like <h1> are supported.
4. Workflow for adding a new page: ① Define view function in views.py; ② Add a new path entry to urlpatterns.

III. 
1. Create a folder named templates at project root to store .html files. templates is Django’s default template directory name.
2. Use render(request, "template_name.html") inside view function to load HTML file. Need import statement: from django.shortcuts import render.
3. Basic HTML tags:
   - <h1> ~ <h6>: heading tags, h1 is largest, size decreases from h1 to h6
   - <p>: paragraph tag
   - <div>: block-level container, takes full width on new line, no default style
   - <span>: inline container, stays in same line, no default font size
4. HTML structure: Standard skeleton <!DOCTYPE html> <html> <head> <body>. Meta info and page title go inside <head>, visible content inside <body>.

IV. Server Start & Access Flow
1. Start dev server: Run configuration in PyCharm, equivalent to python manage.py runserver.
2. Server is ready when console prints Starting development server at http://127.0.0.1:8000/.
3. Visit http://127.0.0.1:8000/xxx/ in browser. Django matches the path string against entries in urlpatterns. If matched, run the corresponding view function.
4. The view returns content, browser renders and shows the page.
